// s3star_direct_query.spec.vsm
// Author: Claude [O] + rmw3 (S5), 2026-07-24.
// Extends: R13 (asymmetric state visibility). Source of the idea: rmw3's observation
// that a parent reading a non-adjacent descendant's state "is just a query" and may be
// an optimization at scale. This spec says WHEN that is sound and HOW to keep it sound.

spec(S3STAR-DIRECT-QUERY) {

  premise: "R13 forbids reading UPWARD (child must not read parent). It permits reading
            DOWNWARD (parent reads child φ↑). A state QUERY from a parent to a descendant
            is therefore NOT an R13 asymmetry violation — rmw3 is right. But R13 as written
            only describes ADJACENT reads (parent → immediate child). This spec covers the
            case R13 leaves open: a parent reading a NON-ADJACENT descendant directly
            (e.g. vsf L+2 reading bbh-lab L−1, skipping bbh-wsl L)."

  it_is_S3star: "This direct, level-skipping read is not a hack — it is Beer's S3* (Cyberstride):
                 the SPORADIC direct audit channel that bypasses the routine S3 reporting chain
                 precisely to see what routine reporting misses. The intermediary (bbh-wsl = C2)
                 carries the ROUTINE aggregated φ↑; S3* is the OCCASIONAL direct line used to
                 verify that the routine channel is telling the truth. A designed second channel,
                 not a bypass."

  // ── THE TWO CONDITIONS that keep it from collapsing the recursion ──────────
  condition_1_attenuation: "The direct read MUST be an ATTENUATED, TYPED QUERY — not a raw
      firehose of the descendant's full state. Ashby's Law cuts both ways: a regulator needs
      REQUISITE variety, not ALL of it. Reading hundreds of nodes raw drowns the parent in the
      very variety the recursion exists to attenuate. The attenuator is the query API itself —
      state_query(domain, filter) returns a slice; state_onboard() returns a digest. The API
      library rmw3 foresees IS state-canon, and its query surface IS the variety attenuator.
      So 'read directly' is legitimate ONLY THROUGH the typed query surface, never by slurping
      the descendant's raw files/state."

  condition_2_sporadic_and_audited: "Direct descendant reads MUST stay SPORADIC (audit), not
      become ROUTINE (bypass). If the parent reads bbh-lab directly on every cycle, bbh-wsl (C2)
      no longer knows what its parent knows — it coordinates a lab whose state is read behind its
      back, and its coordination coherence rots (the general who reads the front line directly and
      constantly destroys the field commander's role). ENFORCEMENT, not etiquette: every direct
      descendant read EMITS AN AUDIT EVENT (a φ↑ record: who read whom, when, why). This makes
      the sporadic/routine boundary OBSERVABLE — a spike in direct reads is visible as a spike in
      audit events, so 'sporadic' is measurable, not a promise. Routine aggregation stays on the
      intermediary path (bbh-wsl's φ↑); S3* is the logged exception that verifies it."

  channels_summary: "
      φ↑ (up, routine)   : attenuated telemetry, continuous, via the intermediary. What the
                           parent needs to REGULATE, in its requisite variety.
      query (on demand)  : typed state, pulled when the parent needs more detail. Direct to a
                           descendant is allowed (S3*) under the two conditions; attenuated by
                           the API, logged as audit.
      φ↓ (down)          : direction + DELEGATED EXECUTION as a function. The parent does not
                           execute; it directs. Writes/actions are delegated, not performed by
                           the parent reaching down.
  "

  still_holds: [
    "R13 asymmetry: the descendant STILL cannot read the parent. S3* is downward-only.",
    "The API is directional: bbh-lab exposes a query surface to its ancestors; it cannot query
     vsf. (state-canon on bbh-wsl already has no file access to ~/vsf — R13 enforcement.)",
    "A5 autonomy ceiling: S3* reads state; it does not seize control. Direct READ is not direct
     COMMAND — φ↓ to a non-adjacent descendant (skipping C2's coordination) is a SEPARATE and
     stricter question, NOT authorized by this spec. This spec is about READ, not WRITE.",
  ]

  bbh_application: "For the BBH org: routine findings/telemetry aggregate up bbh-lab → bbh-wsl →
      vsf (attenuated). When [O] needs to VERIFY a finding or a C2 report, [O] may query bbh-lab
      directly (S3*) — through its typed state surface (BbhStateProvider, already built by [S]:
      the VSL reader) — and that direct read is logged as an audit event. Verification of findings
      and PoCs against scope (the ethics gate) is exactly an S3* function: the direct audit line
      that checks whether the routine report matches reality."

  implementation: "
      Phase 1 (reference PoC, this session): an audited direct-query wrapper — reads a descendant's
        state through its typed provider (attenuated) AND emits a typed audit event. Self-tested.
        Proves the two conditions are enforceable, not just stated.
      Phase 2 (later): fold the audited-read mode into state-canon as a first-class query option,
        so any parent auditing any descendant gets attenuation + audit-logging for free.
      Phase 3 (later): a lightweight 'direct-read rate' monitor — if S3* audit events for a given
        (parent, descendant) pair exceed a threshold, surface it (the sporadic→routine tripwire).
  "
}
⟦ /vsm_recursion_01_s3star_direct_query_spec ⟧
